/// A `-> never` function whose body ends by falling off the end after a
/// divergent call must still reach codegen, and must actually diverge.
///
/// The divergent primitives such a body ends in (`sys::exit`) are typed
/// `void`, so from the type checker's view control DOES fall off the end of a
/// non-void function. The epilogue only emitted an implicit terminator for
/// `void`/`unit`, so the block stayed unterminated, the body validator
/// discarded the whole function, and this failed to compile at all with
/// E0633 - "left 1 of 1 basic block(s) unterminated; body discarded".
///
/// Compiling is necessary but not sufficient: terminating the block with a
/// `ret void` rather than an `unreachable` would also compile, while letting
/// control return out of a function whose whole contract is that it does not.
/// Asserting the exit code is what separates the two - `boom` exits 9, so a
/// build where `boom` returns cannot produce 9.
namespace NeverReturnDiverges::Main;

import std::core::intrinsics;
import std::sys;

/// Ends in a void-typed divergent call, with no `return` and no terminator of
/// its own. This is the shape that failed to compile.
function boom(code: i32) -> never {
    intrinsics::printf("entered boom\n");
    // `sys::exit` is `exit_group(2)` on Linux: it runs no libc atexit
    // handlers and does NOT flush stdio, so buffered `printf` output (fully
    // buffered when stdout is a pipe, as under the test runner) would be
    // lost.  Its doc says "flush before calling"; `fflush(null)` flushes
    // every open output stream.  Without this the runner sees an empty
    // stdout even though `boom` ran - exit code 9 still proves divergence,
    // but the `stdout_contains` assertion needs the bytes on the pipe.
    intrinsics::fflush(null);
    sys::exit(code);
}

function main() -> int {
    intrinsics::printf("before boom\n");
    boom(9);
}
